WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.690
- The second oldest rollercoaster
in the world is this:

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:08.070
the Great Scenic Railway
at Luna Park in Melbourne, Australia.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.600
It was built in 1912.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.390
And it's one of very few
roller coasters in the world

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.240
where the brakes are
still operated manually,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.670
where a brake operator,
like Freya there,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.000
rides along on every run,
timing everything

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.910
and making sure that the
riders go over the hills

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.880
at exactly the right speed
every time.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.650
So obviously, I had some questions

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.170
for Freya and the team here
about how it all works.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.520
But I thought I might want to wait

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.171
until Freya was a little bit less busy!

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.970
- I have been driving for
about five or six years now,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.130
which is a lot of fun.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.170
Sending people around on a rollercoaster

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.640
is a pretty unusual job.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.692
We usually try and keep people
on only for an hour at a time,

00:00:51.692 --> 00:00:53.640
'cause it can
get very tiring, obviously.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.040
It's all manual labor,
slowing it down.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.220
We do about 24 hours'
worth of training,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.920
which includes doing a
walk up the track, theory,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.340
as well as a lot and a lot
and a lot of driving.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.350
Same as driving a car,
trying to expose them

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.590
to as many different
conditions as possible.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.490
- I did not expect to get actual air time
from a 110-year-old rollercoaster.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.840
Wow. That's not a bad
view from up here either!

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.813
- It is so dependent on the conditions
that you're driving on.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:24.570
There's no, "this is exactly how
you drive the train every time".

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.880
Everyone has their own
little ways of doing it.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.820
We obviously don't just throw
someone behind the brake

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.330
and say, "go for it," because
standing up on a rollercoaster

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.820
is a weird thing to do!

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.920
Once they're all comfortable
standing up,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.840
we'll move them behind the brake.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.260
And as the trainer, you will tell them
when to apply and not apply brake.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.560
And from then on it's
just doing a lot of laps

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.120
to get experience in different conditions.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.560
Every cycle you run is different.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.080
You've got to be able to
make those judgment calls.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.130
If it is too windy,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.750
depending on the direction
it's coming from,

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.070
it will stop the train.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.470
So we keep an eye out
on the wind radar

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.240
and things like that
to keep a ballpark,

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.000
as well as, our drivers
have a pretty good idea

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.270
of how that's impacting the train.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.920
Rain, we also don't run in.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.020
It makes the train go way too fast

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.660
and it becomes difficult
to actually slow it down.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.850
If it does come to a stop,

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.620
if it's too windy or
something like that,

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.450
we get a team of people
to come up.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.910
They walk up the track,
which they're all trained how to do.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.190
And then give it a big
push start to get it going,

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.290
which is always a bit of
fun and entertainment

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.450
for all of the guests on board!

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.760
- Sure, this is more than 100 years old,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.120
but it needs to perform to
21st-century safety standards

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.340
and get 21st-century inspections.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.590
So how does it do that?

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:41.315
- Where we run into
modern safety rules,

00:02:41.315 --> 00:02:46.200
we have to negotiate with
the Heritage Council and say,

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.220
"this is the most sensitive way
we can possibly achieve the result".

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.960
It's a difficult balancing act.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.650
And a very, very
expensive one to maintain.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.580
Continuous maintenance.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.673
And by no means is
it off the shelf!

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.450
It's all 1900s equipment.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.530
We have to replace
like-for-like wherever possible.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.990
That means we have to have
them manufactured specially.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.883
It's a never ending job.

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:16.020
So we only run two trains on the track.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.110
The first train that is dispatched

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.290
is required to get
halfway round the track

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.720
before we dispatch the next one.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.980
Now there are four blocks
defined on the track by lights.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.820
Two opportunities on every
circuit to pull the train up.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.730
That was done nigh-on 20 years ago.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.340
Modern legislation,
we had to conceive

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.310
of everything that
could possibly go wrong.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.590
The longer you leave risk,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.693
the more chance it's
got of occurring!

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:50.910
And there have been discussions
to modernize that control system

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:55.410
so that brakes on the train
could be invoked externally.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.090
All of these things are on the list,

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.340
and will eventually have to happen.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.917
No question.

00:04:00.917 --> 00:04:02.557
- Freya, Thank you very much!

00:04:02.557 --> 00:04:04.807
- That's all right.

